---
title: File Transfer
order: 6
description: curl, wget, rsync (remote transfers)
tags: [curl, wget, rsync, download, upload, transfer, http]
---

Tools for downloading, uploading, and synchronizing files over the network.
For rsync over SSH, see also ssh/transfer.

@code bash
# ---- curl: transfer data with URLs ----

# basic downloads
curl https://example.com                         # output to stdout
curl -o file.html https://example.com            # save to file
curl -O https://example.com/file.tar.gz          # save with remote filename
curl -OL https://example.com/file.tar.gz         # follow redirects + save
curl -C - -O https://example.com/large.iso       # resume interrupted download

# HTTP methods
curl -X GET https://api.example.com/data
curl -X POST https://api.example.com/data -d '{"key":"val"}' -H 'Content-Type: application/json'
curl -X PUT https://api.example.com/data/1 -d '{"key":"new"}'
curl -X DELETE https://api.example.com/data/1
curl -X PATCH https://api.example.com/data/1 -d '{"key":"patched"}'

# headers and auth
curl -H 'Authorization: Bearer TOKEN' https://api.example.com
curl -H 'Accept: application/json' https://api.example.com
curl -u user:password https://example.com         # basic auth
curl --negotiate -u : https://example.com         # Kerberos

# POST data
curl -d "name=foo&value=bar" https://example.com/form        # form-urlencoded
curl -F "file=@photo.jpg" https://example.com/upload          # multipart file upload
curl -F "file=@photo.jpg;type=image/jpeg" https://example.com/upload
curl -d @data.json -H 'Content-Type: application/json' https://api.example.com

# useful options
curl -s https://example.com                       # silent (no progress)
curl -sS https://example.com                      # silent but show errors
curl -v https://example.com                       # verbose (debug)
curl -I https://example.com                       # HEAD request (headers only)
curl -w '%{http_code}\n' -o /dev/null -s https://example.com    # status code only
curl -w '%{time_total}\n' -o /dev/null -s https://example.com   # response time
curl -k https://self-signed.example.com           # ignore SSL errors
curl -L https://example.com                       # follow redirects
curl --max-time 10 https://example.com            # timeout
curl --retry 3 https://example.com                # retry on failure

# download multiple files
curl -O https://example.com/file1.txt -O https://example.com/file2.txt
curl -K urls.txt                                  # read options from file

@code bash
# ---- wget: non-interactive downloader ----

# basic downloads
wget https://example.com/file.tar.gz              # download to current dir
wget -O output.html https://example.com            # save with specific name
wget -O - https://example.com                      # output to stdout
wget -c https://example.com/large.iso              # resume download
wget -q https://example.com/file.tar.gz            # quiet mode
wget -b https://example.com/large.iso              # background download

# recursive download
wget -r https://example.com/                       # recursive (mirror)
wget -r -l 2 https://example.com/                  # limit depth to 2
wget -m https://example.com/                       # mirror mode (-r -N -l inf)
wget -r -np https://example.com/docs/              # no parent directory
wget -r -A "*.pdf" https://example.com/            # accept only PDFs
wget -r -R "*.html" https://example.com/           # reject HTML files

# authentication
wget --user=admin --password=secret https://example.com/protected
wget --header='Authorization: Bearer TOKEN' https://api.example.com

# other options
wget --limit-rate=200k https://example.com/file    # throttle download
wget -i urls.txt                                    # download URLs from file
wget --no-check-certificate https://self-signed.example.com
wget --spider https://example.com                  # check if URL exists (no download)
wget -t 5 https://example.com/file                 # retry 5 times
wget -w 2 -r https://example.com/                  # wait 2s between requests

@code bash
# ---- rsync: efficient file synchronization ----

# remote transfers (SSH is default transport)
rsync -avz /local/path/ user@host:/remote/path/         # push to remote
rsync -avz user@host:/remote/path/ /local/path/         # pull from remote
rsync -avz -e 'ssh -p 2222' src/ user@host:/dest/       # custom SSH port

# common flags
# -a  archive mode (recursive, preserves permissions/times/symlinks/owner/group)
# -v  verbose
# -z  compress during transfer
# -P  --partial --progress (resume + show progress)
# -n  dry run (show what would be done)
# -h  human-readable sizes

rsync -avzP /local/path/ user@host:/remote/path/        # archive + progress
rsync -avzn /local/path/ user@host:/remote/path/        # dry run first

# trailing slash matters!
rsync -avz src/ dest/                     # contents of src into dest
rsync -avz src dest/                      # src directory into dest (creates dest/src/)

# delete files on dest that don't exist on source
rsync -avz --delete src/ dest/
rsync -avz --delete-after src/ dest/      # delete after transfer

# exclude patterns
rsync -avz --exclude='*.log' src/ dest/
rsync -avz --exclude='.git' --exclude='node_modules' src/ dest/
rsync -avz --exclude-from='excludes.txt' src/ dest/
rsync -avz --include='*.c' --exclude='*' src/ dest/     # only .c files

# bandwidth limit
rsync -avz --bwlimit=1000 src/ dest/      # limit to 1000 KB/s

# rsync daemon (not over SSH)
rsync -avz rsync://mirror.example.com/repo /local/mirror/

@text
Key differences:
- curl supports many protocols (HTTP, FTP, SMTP, etc.) and is best for
  API interaction and single-file transfers.
- wget excels at recursive downloads and mirroring websites.
- rsync is best for synchronizing directory trees efficiently, as it
  only transfers differences (delta encoding).

All three can resume interrupted downloads (curl -C -, wget -c, rsync --partial).
